<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eritrean Liberation Front</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1960">1960</a>–<a href="/years/1981">1981</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Eritrean Liberation Front was a nationalist movement founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in 1960 by Eritrean exiles to win independence from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>. Its fighters under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamid-idris-awate">Hamid Idris Awate</a> began the armed struggle in September 1961. Divisions within the front led to the breakaway of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front">Eritrean People's Liberation Front</a> in the 1970s, which defeated the ELF in a civil war and drove it into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> in 1981.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eritrean-liberation-front.jpg" alt="Eritrean Liberation Front" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Orange Tuesday Original uploader was Orange Tuesday at en.wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Eritrea_(1952-1961).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Under a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> resolution <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> in 1952 with its own autonomous government. Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> dismantled that autonomy step by step: the Eritrean Trade Union was outlawed in 1953, the National Assembly ceased to exist in 1956, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amharic">Amharic</a> replaced Arabic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigrinya-language">Tigrinya</a> as the official language. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-liberation-movement">Eritrean Liberation Movement</a>, founded in 1958 among students and workers, organised protests but proved unable to move to armed struggle. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> meanwhile trained Eritreans, among them Idris Muhammad Adam, at a camp in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandria">Alexandria</a> modelled on the methods of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algeria">Algeria</a>'s national liberation front.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The ELF was openly founded in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> in July 1960 by Idris Muhammad Adam and other intellectuals and students, mostly Muslim, and it adopted Pan-Arab beliefs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hamid-idris-awate">Hamid Idris Awate</a>, a former soldier of the Italian colonial era, opened the fighting on 1 September 1961 in the western lowlands near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, its safe haven; after his death in June 1962 his group merged with the ELF. Ethiopia annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> on 14 November 1962. Armed by Arab states and others, the front grew to 2,000 fighters by 1965, organised in four, later five, Zonal Commands.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>'s counter-offensive of 1967 and 1968, backed by Israel and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, destroyed hundreds of villages and deepened the split between Muslims and Christians within the front, which saw massacres of Christian fighters. In 1971 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isaias-afwerki">Isaias Afwerki</a> and others broke away, and their factions formed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front">Eritrean People's Liberation Front</a> in August 1973; the ELF began war on them in February 1972. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derg">Derg</a> overthrew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a> in 1974, the two fronts briefly joined in attacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asmara">Asmara</a> in January 1975, but by 1977 the EPLF had overtaken the ELF.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a>, with Soviet help, recovered most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrea">Eritrea</a> in 1978, a failed ELF counter-offensive revived the quarrel with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eritrean-peoples-liberation-front">EPLF</a>. The ELF fled into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>, where it lost its weapons and broke up. The war of independence went on under the EPLF, and Eritrea became fully independent on 24 May 1991.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eritrean Liberation Front” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Nationalists organised the Eritrean Liberation Movement in 1958 and the <b>Eritrean Liberation Front</b> in 1960, whose fighters under Hamid Idris Awate began armed struggle in September 1961.</blockquote><a href="/state/eritrea-federation">Eritrea (federation)</a></li></ul></section>
